; End-to-end proof that a CONFINED sandbox mode actually confines a spawned
; command through the OS backend — the one property the rest of the blackbox
; suite deliberately does not exercise, because test-cases/dune pins
; MENTAT_SANDBOX_MODE=danger-full-access (unconfined, cross-platform) for
; determinism. Each .t here overrides that pin explicitly and drives a real
; `shell` tool call under `mentat run`, so enforcement is observed end to end.
;
; Platform gating. OS confinement is backend-specific: macOS Seatbelt
; (/usr/bin/sandbox-exec) and Linux Bubblewrap (/usr/bin/bwrap) block in
; different ways, and neither runs on the other host. So the enforcing bodies
; are split per backend and gated by %{system}. The mechanism is the same as
; the sibling sandbox/ family: the parent whole-subtree stanza (test-cases/dune)
; already attaches every .t here to the `blackbox` alias with no guard; each
; scoped stanza below ANDs in a %{system} guard, and dune folds the two into one
; spec whose enabled_if is the conjunction. A backend's cases therefore run only
; on that backend's host and are silently skipped — never false-green — on the
; other. On this repo's CI both hosts run, so both backends are covered.
;
; escalation.t is intentionally ungated: it proves the per-command escalation
; PERMISSION shape (a separate shell.escalate access), which is a pure policy
; property independent of which backend enforces, and it pins
; MENTAT_SANDBOX_REQUIRE=off so the run starts and the decision surfaces on any
; host regardless of backend availability.

(cram
 (applies_to seatbelt-write seatbelt-read seatbelt-network)
 (alias blackbox)
 (enabled_if
  (= %{system} macosx)))

(cram
 (applies_to bwrap-write bwrap-read bwrap-network)
 (alias blackbox)
 (enabled_if
  (= %{system} linux)))

; review-seal.t pins the review-mode read-only seal — a posture-owned
; confinement, not a configured one — so its body is deliberately backend- and
; wording-agnostic (filesystem bytes and the evidence marker, no backend names
; or refusal text) and one file serves both enforcing hosts. It is gated to
; their union and skipped where neither backend exists.

(cram
 (applies_to review-seal)
 (alias blackbox)
 (enabled_if
  (or
   (= %{system} macosx)
   (= %{system} linux))))
